            <ArticleTitle>[Study of magnesium level in myocardium and aorta tissues from subjects free of ishemic necrosis (author's transl)].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>101-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Before carrying out a study on the levels of magnesium in the myocardium and the aorta in subjects who had died of coronary thrombosis, we determined the reference levels of magnesium in the right and left ventricles and in the aorta of sixteen normal subjects. The samples were homogenized and digested with nitric acid/hydrochloric acid mixture before the measurement itself was made; this was carried out by atomic absorption spectrophotometry. The magnesium values are expressed in microng/g of wet tissue, in mg/g of tissular proteins, in mg/ of total lipids in the aortic wall. The differences between the concentrations of proteins or magnesium in the left and right ventricles are significant magnesium in the left and right ventricles are significant (P less than 0,001). They probably derive from anatomic and physiological differences. We have found a correlation which is significantly positive between magnesium level and protein level in the ventricle. In the aorta, the results for proteins, lipids and magnesium are extremely dispersed because of the heterogeneous nature of biochemical composition of this tissue according to age and area which it has been taken from.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
